From c45a92101865ef508da8e6f6143fc47fff7ee5f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 18:24:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Draw TextKit 2 text into every scroll tile a pass paints

Scroll layers keep the tiles a display pass draws, including the tile
it draws ahead of a scroll while the compositor shows the frame. A
TextKit 2 text view drew only the fragments its viewport controller had
configured, and its viewport was exactly what showed, so a tile drawn
ahead held the view's background but none of its text. The tile stayed
valid, and when it scrolled into view nothing redrew it unless layout
happened to report damage, which text laid out already never does: a
transcript scrolled back over went blank for seconds, until something
unrelated invalidated it.

A text view now takes what a pass asks it to draw into its viewport
before drawing, as overdraw kept within two heights of what shows (at
least 1024 points), the way AppKit lays out its overdraw. The fragments
there are laid out and configured, then drawn. Layout during drawing
doesn't scroll to keep text in place; viewWillDraw did that before the
pass.

Scrolling no longer sends viewWillDraw down the whole window and walks
every view for bounded layer canvases on each step: the clip views that
scrolled or resized are noted, and only their documents are prepared,
unless something is damaged, when the whole tree gets viewWillDraw as
before.

linux_scroll's new test scrolls a text view in a container down and back
up in small steps and fails if any part of what shows was never painted
with text; it fails without this change.

Signed-off-by: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
